course: dictionary
lesson: 13002
title: Extensive: wide and thorough
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Extensive tells you that something is large or wide-ranging. «The park has extensive gardens, with lawns, lakes and woods.» The clues are lawns, lakes and woods, which all take up a lot of space, so extensive must mean covering a large area.
explain: Extensive can also mean large in amount, or very thorough. «The detective made extensive searches of the house.» She did not just look in one room. She searched many places very carefully. Check your meaning by swapping in big or thorough: if the sentence still makes sense, you are close.
explain: In geography, extensive farming means using a lot of land with few workers for each field. A sheep station in Australia is a good example: it spreads over huge areas, but only a few people work there. This is the opposite of intensive farming, which uses a small area with lots of work or money.
fact: Extensive means covering a large area, or large in amount.
fact: «The flood caused extensive damage to the town.» Here extensive means the damage was large and widespread.
fact: Extensive is an adjective, so it describes a noun: extensive knowledge, extensive damage, extensive grounds.
fact: The forms are extensive, more extensive and most extensive.
fact: In farming, extensive farming uses a lot of land with little work or money per field. It is the opposite of intensive farming.
exercise: «The ancient castle sat in extensive grounds with woods and lakes.» What does extensive mean here? | Made of stone | *Covering a large area | Very short and narrow | Very quiet
exercise: «The scientist did extensive research before she wrote the book.» What does extensive suggest? | Noisy | Brief and careless | Only a little | *Large and very thorough
exercise: «The farm covers an extensive area of hills.» Find and copy the word that means large. | area | hills | farm | *extensive
test: «The map showed the extensive forests of the north.» What does extensive mean? | Very small | Dark and cold | Covered in snow | *Covering a large area
test: Nadia says extensive is just another word for big. Why is extensive a useful word to choose? | It is a noun, so it names things | *It can mean big in area, or big and thorough in amount | It means happy | It means very small
exam: In geography, what does extensive farming mean? | Farms that grow only flowers | Farms with lots of workers on tiny fields | *Big farms using a lot of land with few workers per field | Farms with no land at all
